WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Privileged behavior is strongly influenced by inherited state.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:19.960
A C program may appear to perform a simple file operation, but the result depends on directory ownership, permissions, symbolic links, mount options, current working directory, umask, environment variables, and open descriptors or handles.

3
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.380
These inputs are part of the design even when they are not function parameters.

4
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.660
Filesystem checks should focus on trusted locations and expected properties.

5
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:36.280
Directory ownership, write permissions, and resource permissions determine who can influence files that privileged code reads or writes.

6
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.660
Created files should have intended permissions from the beginning, not rely on cleanup after the fact.

7
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.520
The umask and platform defaults should be considered part of the file creation contract.

8
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.520
Temporary locations need special care.

9
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:58.700
A privileged program should not assume that a generic temporary directory is suitable for sensitive data, controlled outputs, or coordination files.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.600
Use trusted directories, safe creation patterns, restrictive permissions, and predictable cleanup.

11
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.540
The point is not clever path handling; it is eliminating ambiguity around who can influence the resource.

12
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.420
Environment state can change behavior in surprising ways.

13
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:24.080
Variables may affect library loading, configuration discovery, localization, diagnostics, proxy behavior, temporary paths, and child process execution.

14
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:32.580
A privileged program should decide which inherited environment values are accepted, which are ignored, and which are replaced with known values.

15
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.380
Open descriptors or handles also cross privilege boundaries.

16
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:45.200
A process may hold resources that remain accessible even after identity changes, or it may pass resources to children unintentionally.

17
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:53.780
Reviewers should look for descriptor lifecycle, close-on-exec or handle inheritance settings, and cleanup behavior on every error path.

18
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.020
Cleanup must respect the same boundaries as creation.

19
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:04.720
Removing, replacing, or changing files under elevated authority should use trusted paths and verified state.

20
00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.460
Error handling should avoid turning a cleanup routine into a broad privileged action.

21
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.280
Safe cleanup is specific, conservative, and observable.
